Year = {2016},
Volume = {7},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {A coding polymorphism of human ATG16L1 (rs2241880; T300A) increases the
   risk of Crohn's disease and it has been shown to enhance susceptibility
   of ATG16L1 to caspase cleavage. Here we show that T300A also alters the
   ability of the C-terminal WD40-repeat domain of ATG16L1 to interact with
   an amino acid motif that recognizes this region. Such alteration impairs
   the unconventional autophagic activity of TMEM59, a transmembrane
   protein that contains the WD40 domain-binding motif, and disrupts its
   normal intracellular trafficking and its ability to engage ATG16L1 in
   response to bacterial infection. TMEM59-induced autophagy is blunted in
   cells expressing the fragments generated by caspase processing of the
   ATG16L1-T300A risk allele, whereas canonical autophagy remains
   unaffected. These results suggest that the T300A polymorphism alters the
   function of motif-containing molecules that engage ATG16L1 through the
   WD40 domain, either by influencing this interaction under non-stressful
   conditions or by inhibiting their downstream autophagic signalling after
   caspase-mediated cleavage.},
